  "notes": "The lane's guitar-forward modern flagship, and one of the seven models in this section that genuinely carries an 808. The entry says the low end is \"sub-808 or synth bass with a long decay on the studio material — this is where she touches `trap-soul` practice — but played electric bass on the guitar-led and live-band cuts. Slides are rare.\" Both are authored: `drums.bass808` is present with role **counter_riff** and a slide probability of 0.06, which keeps the sub while leaving the bassline generator alive for the played-bass cuts — role `bassline` would have silenced it. Drums are \"soft programmed kit under live-feel percussion — a filtered kick on 1 and the 16th before 3, a low, short clap/snare, quiet 16th hats, and finger-snaps\", with a band-tracked variant of displaced snares, so the snare is layered with a 2–9 ms window that spans both. Harmony is the three families the entry writes out, including the blues I–IV–V \"on the guitar-led material\" — her cited influences are all harmonically blues-rooted. The identifying instrument is the guitar: \"it supplies both the chord bed (clean, chorused, arpeggiated) and the solo\", which is the `arp` counter and the guitar-solo instrumental section in the arrangement. Melody is \"mid-register alto, conversational, moderate note-density, with runs saved for phrase ends\", hence `endVariation` and a density of 3–7. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum probability and velocity and all melody numbers; the research marks the drum and 808 detail as inferred from the records. ⚠ The half-time subset the entry says \"matters as much as the straight one\" is [LOW-CONF] there and is not encoded — one model states one placement.",
